tools/README.md:25:| `pipeline/check_commit_attribution.py` | asks whether the LEDGER noticed the work that SHIPPED. On its first run, 33 of the 40 most recent commits were named by no row -- most legitimately, two sharply: a commit whose subject read "selfmutant6 CLOSED" against a row still marked held, and a row whose `commits` field named an unrelated device round. **Reports BOTH its own blindnesses on every run** -- row ids too short to match safely, and rows carrying commits that no subject ever names -- because a check that silently skips a third of its subjects while printing a green line is this project's newest named vacuity class. |
34990:    "opened": "2026-09-10",
34991:    "title": "cpu_arm_coproc.c arm_coproc_i80321_6: reading the modelled XScale CP6 interrupt-enable register loads the correct value into rd and then calls exit(1) -- while the ADJACENT case 4 reads its own modelled register with no fatal and no exit",
34992:    "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nIn `arm_coproc_i80321_6', crm 0 / crn 0, the load arm reads:\n    cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\");\n    exit(1);\nThe destination register receives the modelled value and the process then dies.  The STORE arm immediately below writes the same field, and the i80321 interrupt controller consumes it, so the register is fully modelled in both directions.\n\n*** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, and it is one screen away: `case 4:' (i80321_isteer) has the IDENTICAL structure -- load arm assigns `cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer;', store arm writes it -- and carries NO fatal() and NO exit(). ***  So crn 0's kill is an OUTLIER inside its own switch rather than a policy, which is the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times: the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing, and the sibling he did not.  That is a far stronger warrant than 'it is a host kill', and it is one grep away -- exactly what the option-set rule's sibling check is for.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_iq80321.c installs it explicitly (`cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;'), and Iyonix does the same.  This is not an unattached helper. Emulated firmware reading its own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no supervisor-only precondition of its own.\n\nDISPOSITION: option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) -- delete the two lines, keep the assignment, with NO latch, for the same reason as [[mapledmaaddr]]: the read is implemented and is not ignored, so an announce would ship a wrong record.  FAULT is unavailable here in any case: memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception for MIPS and M88K only, and this is ARM.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built; the rung-3 attempt is owed.  Note a witness needs a coprocessor read from an ARM guest, which is a different vehicle from the device probes this project mostly writes -- gate_arm.sh's 358 decode checks are the nearest existing machinery.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: ACCEPT IS RATIFIED, BUT MY SIBLING WARRANT WAS OVERCLAIMED AND IS NARROWED HERE.  Verified locally before recording. ***\n\nI wrote that the adjacent `case 4:' (isteer) 'carries NO fatal() and NO exit()'.  **FALSE ABOUT THE WHOLE CASE.**  Its READ arm is quiet, as I said -- but its STORE arm assigns the field and then `exit(1)' on ANY NONZERO VALUE.\n\n**THE ASYMMETRY I RELIED ON SURVIVES, AND ONLY IN THE READ DIRECTION:** case 4 READS quietly while case 0 READS and dies, which is the outlier evidence.  In the WRITE direction the two swap over -- case 0 writes quietly, case 4 writes and dies on nonzero.  So the file has kills in both cases, on opposite halves, and the correct statement is that THE READ HALVES ARE ASYMMETRIC, not that one case is clean and the other is not.\n\nThe verdict stands with a narrower warrant: **ACCEPT for the ordinary interrupt-enable read into a general register.**  Its sibling's write-side kill is a SEPARATE defect on a separate arm and belongs to a separate row, not folded into this one."
