34999:    "title": "dev_sgi_ip32.c DEVICE_ACCESS(crime): any access whose length is not 4 or 8 falls to `fatal(\"crime access len = %i!\"); exit(1)', so ONE guest byte or halfword load to the IP32 memory/interrupt controller ends the emulator -- and this is MIPS, where the FAULT member is actually available",
35000:    "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which listed it among the six E1 predicates.  VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file.\n\nThe handler is a bare `if (len == 8) {...} else if (len == 4) {...} else { fatal(...); exit(1); }'.  There is no other width arm, so EVERY other access length -- 1, 2, and anything wider -- reaches the kill.  A byte load is an ordinary thing for a guest or a PROM to do while probing, and nothing about the device's mapping advertises a width restriction.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_sgi.c installs it with `dev_crime_init(machine, mem, 0x14000000, ...)' on the IP32 machines.  CRIME is the memory and interrupt controller, i.e. one of the first things any IP32 guest touches.\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION IS THE INTERESTING PART, because member 5 (FAULT) IS GENUINELY AVAILABLE HERE, which it usually is not. ***  memory_rw.c converts a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K only -- and this device is on MIPS.  So `return 0' is not a silent decline here: it is a positive statement that the access could not be serviced, delivered to the guest as the bus error real hardware would give.  That is the dev_unreadable.c idiom the option set records, and machine_sgi.c already installs dev_unreadable ten times for exactly this reason -- the PROM and NetBSD probe absent regions and RELY on the failure.  A round here should weigh FAULT against DECLINE rather than defaulting to decline as most of this queue does.\n\nNOT MEASURED, no witness.  The rung-3 attempt is owed and looks cheap: an IP32 machine with a single byte load at 0x14000000 through the debugger."
35001:   },
35002:   {
35003:    "id": "vmedefaultkill",
35004:    "state": "open",
35005:    "sev": "high",
35006:    "opened": "2026-09-10",
35007:    "title": "dev_vme.c: the default arm announces with debug() -- INVISIBLE at default verbosity -- and then calls exit(1), so a guest probing an unimplemented VME2 register kills the emulator with NO VISIBLE REASON; M88K, so the FAULT member is available",
35008:    "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit, listed among the six E1 predicates. VERIFIED LOCALLY.\n\n*** THIS ONE IS WORSE THAN THE USUAL SHAPE AND THE REASON IS THE DIAGNOSTIC LEVEL. ***  Every other unimplemented-register kill in this queue announces with `fatal()', which this fork has measured CANNOT be silenced by -q (debugmsg.c: fatal() has no early-out and goes straight to va_debug).  This one uses **debug()**, which DOES have an early-out -- `if ((quiet_mode && !ss) || v < 0) return;' -- so at default verbosity the message is not printed at all.  **The emulator therefore dies with no output explaining why.**  A user sees the process vanish; a gate sees a non-zero exit and no diagnostic to grep.  That makes this the hardest member of the class to DIAGNOSE, independent of how hard it is to trigger.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_mvme88k.c installs it -- `vme addr=0x%x', VME2_BASE -- unconditionally on a machine this project actively boots (the same constructor attaches the PCC2 the recent rounds are about).  Probing a bus controller's register space is exactly what firmware does.\n\nDISPOSITION: member 5 (FAULT) is AVAILABLE -- M88K is one of the two families for which memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception -- so `return 0' gives the guest the bus error real VME hardware would.  Member 3 (decline + latched announce) is the alternative and would need the announce PROMOTED from debug() to fatal() to be worth anything, which is a second change the round must make deliberately rather than inherit.\n\nNOT MEASURED, no witness; the rung-3 attempt is owed."
